<?xml version="1.0"?>
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:ObjectManager/etc/config.xsd">

    <!-- The read surface a companion module binds to. RunStore also writes; the interface does not. -->
    <preference for="Muon\DevProfiler\Api\RunReaderInterface"
                type="Muon\DevProfiler\Model\Store\RunStore"/>

    <!--
        How many runs to keep. A DI argument rather than a system.xml field: this is a developer
        tool with no admin surface, and a config section would be one more thing to install,
        protect with ACL and explain.
    -->
    <type name="Muon\DevProfiler\Model\Store\RunStore">
        <arguments>
            <argument name="ringSize" xsi:type="number">50</argument>
        </arguments>
    </type>

    <!--
        NO \Proxy ARGUMENTS ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE. This is load-bearing, not an oversight.

        An earlier revision proxied RunFinalizer, RunStore, Filesystem, StoreManagerInterface and
        DesignInterface, to keep this graph from being built on requests where the gate makes it a
        no-op. That optimisation broke the storefront outright whenever `generated/` was empty:

            Undefined array key "Magento\Framework\App\Http" in PluginList.php:174

        A \Proxy is generated code. Instantiating one inside an App\Http plugin call makes the
        object manager generate and autoload a class in the middle of ___callPlugins(), which
        re-enters the object manager and resets PluginList::$_data — so the very next lookup for
        App\Http finds nothing. With DI compiled the proxy already exists on disk, nothing is
        generated, and the bug is invisible. Reproduced both ways: uncompiled with proxies = 500,
        uncompiled without = 200.

        Clearing generated/ is routine in development, and this module is a development tool, so a
        hard 500 in that state is far worse than constructing a few singletons per request. If you
        are tempted to add laziness back here, note that a Factory is generated too, and
        ObjectManager::getInstance() is an anti-pattern the review gate rejects. The correct fix
        would be to stop needing the collaborator at all, not to defer building it.

        The page-side capture is no longer a plugin here at all — it moved to a frontend-scoped
        observer on controller_front_send_response_before (etc/frontend/events.xml), for the same
        underlying reason. Two plugins remain global: StaticResource below, safe uncompiled because
        that entry point does not build the storefront plugin list, and DB\LoggerInterface, which
        has its own note.
    -->
    <type name="Magento\Framework\App\StaticResource">
        <plugin name="muon_devprofiler_static_resource_writer"
                type="Muon\DevProfiler\Plugin\App\StaticResourceWriter"
                sortOrder="1000"/>
    </type>

    <!--
        The SQL collector. Global because LoggerInterface is resolved during the first
        configuration read, before any area exists.

        The real invariant is narrower than "nothing in this graph is plugged", which was the
        earlier claim here and is not true on a stock 2.4.9: App\State IS plugged, by
        Magento_NewRelicReporting, and the plugin target itself resolves to DB\Logger\LoggerProxy,
        which lazily builds the also-plugged Quiet. What must hold is that no dependency in this
        graph is CONSTRUCTED FOR THE FIRST TIME inside ___callPlugins() — because that re-enters the
        object manager and resets PluginList::$_data, which is the documented cause of the
        "Undefined array key Magento\Framework\App\Http" 500 described above.

        App\State satisfies that only because bootstrap builds it before any plugin runs. That is
        incidental, not enforced, so anything added here must be checked against an empty
        generated/ rather than assumed safe.

        Cost, stated plainly rather than implied: this is the module's only always-on hook, it
        applies to every area including adminhtml, REST, GraphQL, cron and consumers, and no other
        module on a stock install plugs LoggerInterface — so the interceptor exists because of this
        line. With the gate settled it is roughly 3.6us per statement, about 1.5ms on a 200-query
        page. An install that cannot accept that should disable the module rather than rely on
        developer mode, which gates the recording but not the dispatch.
    -->
    <type name="Magento\Framework\DB\LoggerInterface">
        <plugin name="muon_devprofiler_query_logger"
                type="Muon\DevProfiler\Plugin\Db\QueryLogger"
                sortOrder="10"/>
    </type>

    <type name="Magento\Framework\Console\CommandListInterface">
        <arguments>
            <argument name="commands" xsi:type="array">
                <item name="muonProfileShow" xsi:type="object">Muon\DevProfiler\Console\Command\ProfileShowCommand</item>
                <item name="muonProfileList" xsi:type="object">Muon\DevProfiler\Console\Command\ProfileListCommand</item>
                <item name="muonProfileClear" xsi:type="object">Muon\DevProfiler\Console\Command\ProfileClearCommand</item>
            </argument>
        </arguments>
    </type>
</config>
